// The 3-functions-per-file rule. The pipeline concatenates every source file
// into one stream, so this is only checkable because the driver injects
// `#file` markers and the parser records, in `pfile`, which file each entry
// of `prog` came from.
//
// The directory-layout rules are a property of the filesystem, which this
// pass has no access to, so they live in the driver (tools/idlayout).

check_files() {
  int i = 0;
  while(i < len((import pfile))) {
    check_file_at(i);
    i = i + 1;
  }
} return void;
given edge_fixture ():() then calls check_file_at:[2] then calls check_file_at:(0) then calls check_file_at:(1)
given many_file_fixture ():() then calls check_file_at:(0) then calls check_file_at:(3)
given full_env ():() then calls check_file_at:[0]

// Reported once per file rather than once per function over the limit.
//
// The `len(fseen) > 0` guard matters: without `#file` markers every function
// is attributed to the same placeholder, so a legal nine-function project
// would look like one nine-function file. Absent markers the rule is simply
// unknowable, and guessing is worse than not checking.
check_file_at(int i) {
  int n = count_file((import pfile)[i]);
  if(first_of_file(i) == 1 && n > 3 && len((import fseen)) > 0) {
    report_funcs((import pfile)[i], n);
  }
} return void;
